From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 14:38:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Handle empty clip regions and the layer budget correctly

An inset() whose opposing insets meet or cross resolves to an empty clip
region, which per css-masking hides the element entirely. The Rect arm
returned None, and the caller reads None as "no clip-path" and pushes no
layer at all, so the element painted at full size: the exact inverse of
what an empty region means. Clamp the far edges to the near ones so the
rect stays empty instead of inverting into a mirrored region.

inset(50%) is one of the spellings visually-hidden labels use, so it
rendered the label at full size. The other, `clip: rect(0,0,0,0)`, is
handled by upstream's CSS 2 `clip` support.

LayerManager checked `layers_used` against LAYER_LIMIT, but
`layers_used` is only ever incremented: `maybe_pop_layer` gives back
`layer_depth` and never `layers_used`. The check therefore capped the
total number of clips a paint pass may ever push, not how many are open
at once, so a heavy page exhausts it partway through and every later
clip is silently refused. A refused clip does not skip its content; it
paints it unclipped. On a page scrolled to the bottom, 1x1 `clip:
rect(0,0,0,0)` screen-reader spans inside a fixed header painted their
text over the page. Fixed subtrees are hoisted and painted last, which
is why they were the ones left without budget.

Depth is also the quantity worth bounding: each open layer costs the
rasterizer a scratch buffer, and a pop releases it. 1024 concurrently
open layers is still far beyond anything real content nests.

Also fix the depth high-water mark, which read
`layer_depth.max(layer_depth)` (a no-op) instead of writing the
`layer_depth_used` field that exists for it.

An empty clip region discards every pixel below it, but the painter
still pushed the layer and rasterized the whole subtree into it before
the clip threw the result away. On 1400 stacked visually-hidden spans
(the `clip: rect(0,0,0,0)` plus `clip-path: inset(50%)` idiom) at
1280x900 that cost 40.6 ms/frame against a 14.1 ms visibility:hidden
floor: 87% of the price of painting the text, for zero visible pixels.

Skipping is pixel-identical: everything below the early-out paints
inside the layer whose region is empty, so it contributes nothing
anyway. Descendants that leave the box are covered too. A re-homed
abspos still paints from its flow parent, so it is inside the layer and
already invisible, and a position:fixed box is hoisted to be a layout
child of the root with no paint_parent_override, so it never entered
this subtree. Hit testing walks paint_children separately, so it is
untouched.

One difference: past LAYER_LIMIT a refused clip painted its content
unclipped and visible, and now it stays hidden. That is what an empty
region means, and it only matters 1024 open layers deep, where the page
is already mis-rendering everything else.

Upstream already returns early for an empty `clip: rect()`, so this only
adds the clip-path half. Outline and outset box shadow paint outside the
clip-path layer here, so they stay above the early-out. Clipping them
away would be the spec behaviour, but that is a separate change, not
part of an optimisation.
